\b How to create and start AVD (Emulator) without Android Studio GUI App\b0\par
\par
\par
Note: On windows cmd you can use sdkmanager.bat on mac or linux sdkmanager in the commands\par
\par
\cf2 $ sdkmanager.bat --list\par
\par
$ sdkmanager.bat "build-tools;33.0.0"  \par
\par
$ sdkmanager.bat "platforms;android-33"\par
\par
$ sdkmanager.bat "system-images;android-33;google_apis;x86_64"\par
\par
$ sdkmanager.bat create avd --name 'AVD' -- package "system-images;android-33;google_apis:x86_64" \par
\par
$ sdkmanager.bat "sources;android-33"\par
\cf0\par
\par
Add path of folders platforms, platform-tools and emulator in Path env variable\par
\par
\par
Now use avdmanager available in cmdline-tools/bin (same place where sdkmanager is)\par
\par
avdmanager\par
\par
avdmanager list\par
\par
avdmanager list avd\par
\par
avdmanager create avd -n myavd1 -k \ldblquote system-images;android-33;google_apis;x86_64\rdblquote\par
\par
Here\par
\par
-n is the name of the AVD, we can give any name as you like, can also use --name\par
\par
-k is the system image that we downloaded using sdkmanager above, can also use --package\par
\par
(using this command - sdkmanager "system-images;android-33;google_apis;x86_64" )\par
\par
\par
Can also give the id in -d flag\par
\par
avdmanager create avd -n myavd1 -d 17 -k \ldblquote system-images;android-33;google_apis;x86_64\rdblquote\par
\par
The id for a specific device can be referred by running avdmanager list\par
\par
\par
After this create AVD using emulator (emulator executable file is present in emulator folder)\par
\par
emulator -list-avds\par
\par
emulator -avd myavd1\par
\par
Can also start AVD with a specific resolution (size) using -skin option\par
\par
emulator -avd myavd1 -skin 450x750\par
\par
\par
To start AVD in a new state (w/o maintaining the old state)\par
\par
emulator -avd avd_name -no-snapshot-load\par
\par
